+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: koto
+
+Property-based tests were added with `hegeltest` to `crates/koto/tests/koto_tests.rs`
+(module `hegel_properties`), treating the full pipeline (lexer → parser → compiler → VM)
+as the system under test via the public `Koto` API. All test interpreters use a 500ms
+execution limit (the documented `KotoSettings::with_execution_limit` mechanism) so that
+accidentally-looping generated scripts become ordinary errors.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **`arithmetic_matches_rust_oracle`** — Random `+ - * / %` expression trees over i64
+   leaves (small values mixed with the full range, including an `i64::MIN` construction),
+   fully parenthesized, evaluated by Koto and compared bit-for-bit against a Rust oracle.
+   Evidence for the oracle: `KNumber` in `crates/runtime/src/types/number.rs` (int ops wrap,
+   `/` is always float, mixed ops are f64) plus the language guide (`9 / 2 == 4.5`) and the
+   VM's documented `n % 0 → NaN` special case (`run_remainder` in `crates/runtime/src/vm.rs`).
+2. **`operator_precedence_matches_explicit_parenthesization`** — Flat chains like
+   `1 + 2 * 3 ^ 4` (1–7 operators from `+ - * / % ^`) must evaluate identically to the
+   explicitly parenthesized form built from the parser's precedence table
+   (`operator_precedence()` in `crates/parser/src/parser.rs`: `^` > `* / %` > `+ -`,
+   all left-associative).
+3. **`compound_assignment_matches_binary_op`** — `x = a; x op= b; x` must equal `a op b`
+   for all six compound assignment operators over random int/float operands.
+   **Found a real bug** (see below); the known-buggy input (`%=` with integer `0`) is
+   excluded from the property and pinned by a dedicated failing test.
+4. **`comparisons_match_rust_oracle`** — All six comparison operators on random int/float
+   pairs vs a Rust oracle (int/int exact, mixed compared as f64, per `KNumber`'s
+   `Ord`/`PartialEq`).
+5. **`number_display_roundtrip`** — A number's display output re-evaluated as a script
+   yields the same number, with the exact representation (int stays int, float bits
+   preserved). Exception documented in-test: `i64::MIN` displays as
+   `-9223372036854775808`, whose unsigned literal part overflows i64 and reparses as an
+   equal-comparing f64 (int-ness lost; value equality still asserted).
+6. **`string_literal_roundtrip`** — Arbitrary Unicode text rendered as a single-quoted
+   Koto literal (everything non-trivial `\u{...}`-escaped) evaluates back to the original
+   string. Exercises the parser's escape handling (`escape_string_character`).
+7. **`string_concatenation_matches_rust`** — `'a' + 'b'` on arbitrary Unicode strings
+   matches Rust string concatenation.
+8. **`arbitrary_text_never_panics`** — `compile_and_run` on arbitrary Unicode text
+   returns `Ok`/`Err`, never panics or aborts.
+9. **`token_soup_never_panics`** — Same, on koto-shaped token soup drawn from a ~90-token
+   vocabulary (keywords, meta keys, operators, literals, indentation-bearing separators),
+   which reaches parser/compiler paths that random text cannot.
+10. **`token_soup_runs_deterministically`** — The same source run on two fresh interpreters
+    yields the same outcome (type + rendered value, or same error); timeout outcomes are
+    excluded since the execution limit is wall-clock based.
+11. **`modestly_nested_source_never_panics`** — Six nesting shapes (parens, lists, maps,
+    method chains, `not`-chains, nested lambdas) at depths ≤ 32 never panic. The depth
+    bound protects the test process from the known stack-overflow bug below, not any
+    documented contract.
+
+Liveness was verified by temporarily inverting assertions in
+`arithmetic_matches_rust_oracle` and (inside the comparison guard) in
+`token_soup_runs_deterministically`; both failed with minimal shrunk counterexamples
+(`Leaf(I(0))`, empty script), confirming the tests and the determinism-guard branch are live.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Default run (100 cases/property): **all 11 properties pass**; the single intentionally
+  failing pinned test `remainder_assign_by_zero_panics_known_failure` fails
+  deterministically; two `#[ignore]`d tests document the process-aborting bug.
+- Exploratory high-count run at 10x (1000 cases/property, temporarily instrumented with a
+  per-test case counter): every property confirmed to reach case 1000 (the nesting sweep
+  exhausts its 198-combination input space at ~175 cases) — **no additional failures**.
+- The rest of the `koto` crate's suite (`cargo test -p koto --no-fail-fast`) stays green.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### 1. `x %= 0` panics the interpreter (real bug, pinned failing test)
+
+- **Repro:** `Koto::default().compile_and_run("x = 1\nx %= 0\nx")` panics with
+  `attempt to calculate the remainder with a divisor of zero`. Pinned by
+  `hegel_properties::remainder_assign_by_zero_panics_known_failure`, found by
+  `compound_assignment_matches_binary_op`.
+- **Code path:** `run_remainder` in `crates/runtime/src/vm.rs` special-cases an integer
+  zero divisor and evaluates `n % 0` to NaN (lines ~1809–1813), but `run_remainder_assign`
+  (lines ~1896–1905) passes the raw `|a, b| a % b` closure to `run_compound_assign_op`,
+  so `KNumber::rem` reaches `i64::wrapping_rem(0)`, which panics. An untrusted script can
+  therefore take down the host process. Oracle-independent (panic), certainly real.
+- **Expected fix direction (not applied):** mirror the zero-divisor guard in
+  `run_remainder_assign`.
+
+### 2. Deeply nested source overflows the stack during compilation (real bug, `#[ignore]`d reproducer)
+
+- **Repro:** compiling `"(".repeat(1000) + "0" + ")".repeat(1000)` aborts the process with
+  SIGABRT (stack overflow). Reproducer:
+  `hegel_properties::deeply_nested_parens_overflow_the_stack_known_failure` (`#[ignore]`d
+  because there is no way to assert on a process abort), plus an env-driven probe
+  `deep_nesting_probe`.
+- **Thresholds measured** (debug build, 2MiB test-thread stack): nested parens, lists
+  (`[[[…]]]`), maps (`{a: {a: …}}`) and `not`-chains abort between depth 50 and 100;
+  nested lambdas (`|| || … 0`) between 40 and 50. Method chains (`0.abs().abs()…`) are
+  parsed iteratively and survive depth 3200+. Bigger stacks raise the threshold, but the
+  recursion is unbounded.
+- **Code path:** the recursive-descent parser (`parse_expression` and friends in
+  `crates/parser/src/parser.rs`) has no recursion-depth limit; the overflow occurs inside
+  `Koto::compile`, i.e. before any execution limit can apply, so `compile` on untrusted
+  input is not safe. Classic missing-depth-limit interpreter bug.
+
+### Quirk (not pinned as a bug)
+
+- `i64::MIN` displays as `-9223372036854775808`, but reparsing that text yields the float
+  `-9223372036854775808.0` (the unsigned literal `9223372036854775808` overflows i64 and
+  falls back to f64 before negation is applied). The values compare equal, but the
+  int-ness is lost, so display→parse is not variant-preserving at exactly this one value.
+  Documented in `number_display_roundtrip`.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Strict display fixpoint for numbers** (display → parse → display is identity) —
+  weakened to value-equality plus variant-preservation-except-`i64::MIN` after the
+  `i64::MIN` literal-overflow behaviour above turned out to be a parser design consequence
+  rather than something the docs promise.
+- **Full outcome equality in the determinism property** — comparing rendered errors when a
+  timeout is involved is unsound (the execution limit is wall-clock based), so timeout
+  outcomes are excluded from comparison rather than asserted equal.
+- No other properties were abandoned; `^` (power) was deliberately kept out of the
+  Rust-oracle *tree* test (its int/int semantics — exponent truncated to `u32`, wrapping —
+  are implementation-defined rather than documented) but is exercised by the
+  precedence and compound-assignment consistency properties, which need no external oracle.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The skill's guidance that interpreters' highest-yield bugs are unbounded recursion and
+  missing zero/overflow guards was exactly right: both real bugs found here are instances
+  of those two classes, and both were found within the first probe/run.
+- The "probe recursion depth directly, sweeping upward" advice was essential — the
+  overflow threshold (≈50–100 in debug) is low enough that uniform generation might have
+  hit it accidentally *inside* a passing suite and taken the whole process down; probing
+  in a separate process per depth (env-var-driven `#[ignore]`d test) was a clean pattern
+  and might be worth documenting explicitly in the skill for process-aborting bug classes.
+- The oracle-independent vs oracle-dependent weighting was useful for triage: both pinned
+  bugs are oracle-independent (panic/abort), so no time was spent second-guessing oracles.
+- Rust reference gotchas that mattered: recursive composites don't compile (used the
+  documented `fn(depth) -> BoxedGenerator` + `compose!` pattern), `sampled_from` requiring
+  owned `Vec`s, and the note that a deliberate liveness failure leaves a `.hegel` DB entry
+  (deleted afterwards). All accurate.
+- One friction point: there is no per-test teardown hook, so the "confirm the 10x run with
+  a case counter" step required temporary instrumentation that had to be reverted by hand;
+  a built-in case-count report (even at `Verbosity::Normal`) would remove that step.
